;; bnei.dev — BIND zone file, reconciled against the live Cloudflare zone
;; on 2026-08-11 after the migration completed (ADR-0033).
;;
;; This is now a RECORD OF LIVE STATE, not just an import file. It was
;; originally written to seed Cloudflare's "Import DNS records"; the zone has
;; since diverged (mail moved off Mailgun entirely), so it is re-derived from
;; what is actually published. See docs/runbook-dns-cloudflare-migration.md.
;;
;; SOA and apex NS are deliberately omitted — Cloudflare manages both.
;; Authoritative NS are brady.ns.cloudflare.com / sasha.ns.cloudflare.com.
;;
;; !! All A records are DNS-only (grey cloud). !!
;; A proxied record terminates TLS at Cloudflare's edge, which breaks Traefik's
;; TLS-ALPN-01 renewal for that host — silently, up to 90 days later.

$TTL 300

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Web — 4 records replacing the 19 flat per-host A records of the old zone.
;;
;; Three wildcards, not one. The naive reading is "wildcards match exactly one
;; label", but the real rule (RFC 4592) is about the *closest encloser*: a
;; wildcard is only skipped when some nearer node exists. `deep.sub.bnei.dev`
;; does resolve off `*.bnei.dev`, because `sub.bnei.dev` is not a node. But
;; `ente.bnei.dev` and `e2e.bnei.dev` DO exist as A records, and an existing
;; node blocks the parent wildcard for everything beneath it — hence the two
;; extra wildcards. Deleting either A record would change this behaviour.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bnei.dev.                     300  IN  A      82.65.231.50
*.bnei.dev.                   300  IN  A      82.65.231.50
*.ente.bnei.dev.              300  IN  A      82.65.231.50
*.e2e.bnei.dev.               300  IN  A      82.65.231.50

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Inbound mail — Cloudflare Email Routing, catch-all @bnei.dev -> a personal
;; Gmail account. Replaces the previous Mailgun MX records.
;;
;; MX is what makes *forwarding* work at all: a redirect is still a receive,
;; so some server has to accept the message before relaying it. With no MX,
;; senders bounce immediately and the forward never runs.
;;
;; Squarespace's own email forwarding is NOT in use and cannot be: it only
;; activates when the domain uses Squarespace nameservers, and this domain has
;; always been on custom nameservers (googledomains, now Cloudflare).
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bnei.dev.                     300  IN  MX  44 route2.mx.cloudflare.net.
bnei.dev.                     300  IN  MX  50 route1.mx.cloudflare.net.
bnei.dev.                     300  IN  MX  70 route3.mx.cloudflare.net.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Outbound mail — SMTP2GO. (Mailgun is fully retired; it previously held both
;; the MX and the SPF include.)
;;
;; EXACTLY ONE v=spf1 record is permitted. Two is a permerror, which makes SPF
;; fail outright — and with p=reject below, that means rejected mail. If
;; Cloudflare offers to add its own SPF record, edit this one instead.
;; 2 DNS lookups against a limit of 10; both includes are bare ip4/ip6 lists.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bnei.dev.                     300  IN  TXT    "v=spf1 include:spf.smtp2go.com include:_spf.mx.cloudflare.net ~all"

;; aspf=r, not =s, and deliberately: SMTP2GO's Return-Path is the subdomain
;; em726320.bnei.dev, which can never satisfy STRICT SPF alignment. Under
;; aspf=s the SPF leg was structurally dead and DMARC rode on DKIM alone.
;;
;; rua points at this domain, not directly at the Gmail account: an external
;; rua destination requires the receiving domain to publish an authorization
;; record (`bnei.dev._report._dmarc.gmail.com`, RFC 7489 §7.1), which Gmail
;; does not do — so reports would have been silently refused. Same-domain
;; needs no authorization, and the catch-all above delivers it to Gmail anyway.
_dmarc.bnei.dev.              300  IN  TXT    "v=DMARC1; p=reject; sp=reject; adkim=s; aspf=r; rua=mailto:dmarc@bnei.dev"

;; SMTP2GO custom return-path, link tracking, and DKIM. These MUST be explicit
;; records: `*.bnei.dev` above would otherwise answer em726320/link with an A
;; record, and SMTP2GO's verifier would report the domain as misconfigured
;; rather than unconfigured. An explicit record always beats a wildcard.
em726320.bnei.dev.            300  IN  CNAME  return.smtp2go.net.
link.bnei.dev.                300  IN  CNAME  track.smtp2go.net.
s726320._domainkey.bnei.dev.  300  IN  CNAME  dkim.smtp2go.net.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Known divergence from live, deliberate: `smtp._domainkey.bnei.dev` is still
;; published live with Mailgun's old 1024-bit RSA key. It is omitted here
;; because it should be deleted — it is a valid signing key for a provider no
;; longer in use. Harmless while it sits there, but it is one compromised
;; Mailgun account away from being a working spoofing vector.
;;
;; A `*._domainkey` wildcard publishing "v=DKIM1; p=" (an explicit revocation
;; of every unlisted selector) existed briefly and has been removed. It is a
;; sound anti-spoofing technique ONLY if every selector actually in use is
;; published explicitly first — while it was live it revoked SMTP2GO's key.
;;
;; TTLs are still the 300s cutover value. Raise to 3600+ now that the
;; migration is confirmed; there is no longer any reason to pay for 300.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
